I wrote `src/fom_solver.c` and `make verify` passes: it prints `VERIFY OK`. The worst error over the 8 samples is 6.3e-11, against a tolerance of 1e-6. All the operator checks report zero error.

How the solver works:
- **Setup and reset:** `fom_setup` and `fom_update_params` both reset the state to `FOM_STATE_INIT`.
- **Parameter term:** the `t0_p_p0 @ p0` term doesn't change between iterations. So I compute it once per solve with `fom_apply_t0_p_p0` into `work->acc` and pass it as the bias.
- **Each iteration:** one call to `fom_step_t0`, with `fom_sched_gamma(it)` read into a local at the top of the loop. That single call does both matrix products and the soft threshold.
- **State update:** SPEC marks both `su` matrices as pure copies (the new state is `[x[50..99], t0]`). So I skip the matvec and do the copy in place in one loop. That same loop computes the residual `max_abs(new_state - state)`, which avoids a second state buffer.
- **Termination:** with `tol = 0` and `FOM_USE_TOL 0` there is no early exit. The loop always runs `settings.max_iter` iterations and returns `FOM_MAX_ITER_REACHED`.

It meets the embedded constraints: no allocation, no variable-length arrays, no `/` operator and no hardcoded numbers. Matrix products only go through the provided `fom_ops` helpers.

I didn't run a benchmark or try further speedups. Nearly all the time is in `fom_step_t0`, which has to go through the provided kernel anyway.
